Introduction
Metal powders are the basis of many metallurgical processes with numerous applications such as thermal spray coatings, additive manufacturing, sintering, compaction, chemical catalysts, rocket propellants, and soldering of metallic parts (Ref 1). The emerging fields of technology demand specifically tailored materials in the form of powder with specific size, porosity, shape, and morphology (Ref 2). Metal powders can be produced through physical, chemical, or mechanical methods (Ref 2). High-pressure gas atomization (HPGA) is a physical method with wide industrial applications. HPGA takes advantage of the high momentum obtained from a cold, high velocity gas stream to disintegrate the hot molten metal flowing out of a nozzle and turn it into small particles through instability mechanisms. In addition, the expanding gas helps the solidification process of the molten particles to form a fine powder. The quality of the end product, in terms of grain size, distribution, and porosity, depends on the in-flight cooling history of the powder particles (Ref 3). HPGA is a favorite method for powder manufacturers mainly due to versatility, quality, purity, high sphericity, and high solidification rate of the obtained powder (Ref 3). The superior solidification rate promotes microstructure refinement which is not attainable by other methods (Ref 4). This is particularly beneficial for reducing elemental segregation in highly alloyed materials where microstructure refinement and alloy homogeneity are desirable. Furthermore, the spherical morphology of the powder is ideal for industries such as the rapidly growing 3D printing, metal injection molding, and thermal spray coating technologies (Ref 4). The two most common types of HPGA atomizers for powder production are close-coupled and free-fall designs, as shown in Fig. 1. In the free-fall design, the liquid melt falls freely for some distance (typically around 100–300 mm) before interacting with the high velocity gas stream. The free-fall design generally produces a coarser spray
compared to close-coupled atomizers. In close-coupled atomization, on the other hand, the melt meets the gas stream immediately after exiting the nozzle. This design is more favorable for producing fine powders due to close proximity of the atomizing gas to the melt stream which results in a more effective breakup mechanism (Ref 4). However, the close-coupled atomization is prone to freezing of the melt at the nozzle and its operation is difficult, to the extent that close-coupled atomization is considered by many to be more an art rather than a science. The difficulty mainly lies in the general lack of understanding of the gas recirculation effects within the atomization zone and how it relates to initial disruption and distribution of the melt flow prior to melt disintegration (Ref 4). Overall, the melt atomization process in HPGA can be explained by two main steps. In the first step, the melt stream close to the nozzle tip is disrupted by the operating gas. Once the disturbances on the surface grow to critical levels, the molten liquid is disintegrated into large ligaments and droplets. This process is known as primary breakup. The resulting large droplets may undergo secondary breakup to create a plume of fine molten droplets in the second step. The secondary breakup typically occurs within 10 nozzle diameters downstream of the atomizer exit (Ref 2). Many studies have reported the existence of large un-atomized droplets that survived the secondary breakup together with small micro-sized droplets at the same location. This is an indication of melt mass loading effect (Ref 2). It is reported that an increase in melt flow rate weakens the strength of the atomization gas flow at its core, thereby reducing the atomization efficiency. In addition, it was shown by Zeoli et al. Ref 5 that at low gasto-liquid mass flow ratios (GMR) (typically in the range of 1 B GMR B 2), a visible liquid column would protrude outside the nozzle orifice before disintegration (jet atomization). In contrast, at higher GMRs (GMR C 3.3), a thin liquid film develops on the nozzle tip which is then sheared off by high velocity gas stream (pre-filming atomization). Despite the widespread application of HPGA in many industries, the application of HPGA in metal powder production is not fully understood (Ref 6). This is due to the fact that in this process, gas and melt velocity, pressure, and temperature experience large gradients that makes direct numerical simulation of gas and melt interaction extremely difficult. As a result, our understanding of melt atomization is largely based on flow visualization techniques (Ref 7–9). Numerous studies have shown that the characteristics of the powder in HPGA are heavily influenced by the properties of the gas and liquid phases and that the atomizer design plays an important role in how these phases interact (Ref 10).
