Although the numerical models for secondary breakup have been developed and implemented in the form of computational fluid dynamics (CFD) programs since the 1980s, there is still a lack of adequate primary breakup models in this field. The secondary atomization has been successfully modeled in a Lagrangian frame of reference to overcome computational limitations (Ref 11, 12). However, resolving the primary breakup numerically is a daunting task as it requires extensive computational resources. This is because the computational grid has to be fine enough to capture the small physical length scales in an atomization process. To make the matter worse, the combination of very fine cell size and high velocities of the gas phase makes CFD simulation a very slow and timeconsuming process that is not typically available outside academic and research communities. Therefore, there has been little interest among CFD specialists and modeling engineers to simulate the primary breakup for general industrial projects. In this work, we studied the metal atomization process in a high-pressure gas close-coupled atomizer. A numerical model was set up using the ANSYS-FLUENT software to simulate the interaction of the supersonic, turbulent flow of atomization gas with molten particles. A new technique was implemented in combination with an Eulerian-Lagrangian particle tracking approach to simulate the solidification of molten particles due to cooling by the atomization gas. Several experiments were also performed with various operating conditions such as atomization gas pressure, nozzle diameter, and melt flow rate. The results obtained from experiments and simulations were analyzed by looking at gas flow characteristics as well as the correlation of particle size, temperature, and velocity distribution. Based on the variations of pressure and shear stress on the surface of the nozzle and the flow patterns in front of the nozzle, a theory is proposed to explain the effect of atomization gas pressure on the performance of the atomizer and melt flow rate.
