The ‘‘Coupled’’ pressure-velocity scheme in FLUENT was employed for solving the gas flow. Overall, in our simulations, the coupled scheme showed significantly better convergence and robustness in comparison to the SIMPLE family of algorithms and reached a steady-state solution with fewer iterations.
General Particle Motion
Modeling multiphase flows and in particular primary atomization is a daunting task. One of the major problems with spray atomization is that there are many different length scales involved in such an application. That is, a liquid film of some hundred microns may be formed from a nozzle with dimensions in millimeter scale. Later, this film breaks up into droplets of a few hundred to sub-micron size that travel distances in the meter length scales. Employing Eulerian methods such as volume-of-fluid (VOF), specifically with high velocity gas, is not practical. This is because solving the gas phase drastically increases computational time. Also, smaller dimensions in closed-coupled atomization together with larger nominal gas velocities put a severe restriction on cell size and numerical time step. Needless to say that mesh dependency is also imposing another problem on modeling primary atomization. For pressure swirl atomizers, there are an abundance of correlations for estimating the sheet thickness such as the ones proposed in Ref 1, 15. Unfortunately, for close-coupled atomizers, there are only few correlations available in the literature. For instance, Liu Ref 16 proposed a twostage fragmentation process for close-coupled atomization wherein a single phase, turbulent gas flow field is simulated to obtain gas velocity distribution and location of
recirculation areas. This approach requires some prior estimation of the film thickness to obtain the appropriate gas velocity derivative and has mesh size dependency which is not desirable. There are some experimental correlations available for film thickness in references (Ref 17 and 18). For instance, Unal Ref 17 reported a correlation for film thickness that depends on liquid mass flow rate, density, viscosity, and nozzle diameter. But this method does not take into account the effect of gas flow on the atomization process; especially that in close-coupled atomizers, the liquid flow is generally aspired by a combination of suction and shear effects due to the high velocity gas stream and gravity effects. An experimental investigation reported primary droplet sizes in the range of 10%–100% of the nozzle diameter (Ref 19). Some researchers in Ref 2, 3 and 21, injected droplets in the range proposed by Stefan et al. Ref 19 for their closecoupled atomizers. Unfortunately, the physics of close-coupled atomization is not very well studied and understood. In the absence of some universal model that can sufficiently explain the prefilming atomization in these scales, we decided to take a similar approach to Ref 2, 3 and 20, and inject 10 classes of primary droplets at the corner of the nozzle lip where the gas meets the melt flow. This injection point is denoted in Fig. 3 by a cross mark. The boundary condition for the nozzle exit is set as a wall with a temperature equal to that of the melt (see Table 1).
