To simulate the motion of molten particles in this work, the discrete phase model (DPM) in FLUENT was employed which is based on an Eulerian–Lagrangian approach for simulating sprays. In this approach, while the main phase is treated as a continuum (for which NavierStokes equations are solved), the dispersed phase is solved by tracking a large number of discrete particles within the continuum phase. Since the mass loading effect in metal powder applications is high, the continuum phase is twoway coupled with the disperse phase through exchange of momentum and energy. This coupling is modeled numerically through addition of momentum and energy source terms, i.e., Fx, Fy, and Sh, to Equations (2), (3), and (6), respectively. At the end of each particle time step, the corresponding source terms are updated before the next iteration of the continuum phase begins. It should be noted that in discrete phase modeling, specially when the two phases are coupled, the specified mass flow rate of the dispersed phase can be converted to a number of particles injected per unit time. However, tracking individual particles in this fashion is computationally too expensive. To solve this problem, the DPM model instead tracks a number of ‘‘parcels.’’ Each parcel is a group of particles with the same size, velocity, temperature, and physical properties. The motion of a single particle, either liquid or solid, in a Lagrangian frame of reference is described as Ref 13:
