where qp and dp are the particle density and diameter, respectively, l is dynamic viscosity of the gas phase, Cd is the drag coefficient, and Re is the relative particle Reynolds number. The drag coefficient Cd is a crucial parameter for accurate modeling of sprays. Liu et al. Ref 21 presented the Dynamic Drag Force model that is applicable to almost any circumstance. While droplet distortion is ignored in other works, in this model, a spherical droplet can change its shape from a complete sphere to a flat disk at large Weber numbers in extreme cases. It is worth mentioning that the majority of drag coefficient models are proposed for nonmetallic solutions (e.g., water, diesel, gasoline, etc.) that remain in liquid state throughout the spraying process.
During solidification of metal particles, solid phases may appear within droplets that would affect droplet deformation and breakup in a manner which is not well-understood. Nonetheless, Li and Fritsching Ref 1 argued that most of the droplet breakup events end before solidification takes place. As such, employing dynamic drag model and common secondary breakup models is justified. Furthermore, we employed the stochastic tracking (random walk) to simulate particle dispersion in the field. The random walk model includes the effect of instantaneous turbulent velocity fluctuations on the particle trajectory through the use of stochastic methods (Ref 1, 13).
Secondary Atomization and Particle Breakup
As a molten droplet travels inside a fluid, the aerodynamic forces acting on the droplet cause growth of instabilities on the droplet surface. Once the amplitude of these instabilities exceed a certain limit, the droplet breaks up to new satellite droplets, leading to melt atomization. In this study, we used the Kelvin–Helmholtz and Rayleigh–Taylor (KHRT) breakup model (based on the works of Beale and Reitz Ref 22 and Patterson and Reitz Ref 23) that is well suited for transitional Weber numbers and high-speed injection applications. KHRT model is provided in ANSYS-FLUENT as an alternative secondary droplet breakup model that includes instability growth by aerodynamic forces (Kelvin–Helmholtz waves) as well as due to acceleration of droplets ejected into freestream conditions (Rayleigh–Taylor waves). It should be noted that in reality, both mechanisms coexist and the breakup occurs due to the fastest growing instability based on local conditions. Nevertheless, in the KHRT model, only aerodynamic breakup is considered within the core region based on the Wave breakup model. Outside the liquid core region, both KH and RT effects coexist and, as a result, both of them are considered for breakup. Nevertheless, the RT instability is dominant when droplet acceleration is large as in high Weber number sprays. The details for the KHRT model are explained in detail elsewhere (Ref 13) and are not repeated here.
