Material Properties
Variable properties for the atomization gas (here nitrogen) were taken from Bergman et al. Ref 25. Stainless steel powder (alloy 316L, Xi’an New Castle Peak Metal Material Co., Ltd., China) was chosen for the studies in this work as the feed material. Since the temperature of the particles change significantly as they travel inside the spray chamber, the use of variable thermo-physical properties for the simulations was necessary. It should be noted that the composition of stainless steel 316L alloys provided by different suppliers is not identical. Typically, the percentage of molybdenum and sulfur varies from one supplier to another. This can significantly affect the material properties (such as density, viscosity, solidus and liquidus temperature, surface tension, etc.) in the molten state. The composition of the alloy used in this work is summarized in Table 2, as provided by the manufacturer.
A thorough search in the literature was carried out to find a database with the closest composition to the one used in this work. Some references were found which had a different Mo and S percentage (e.g., Ref 26, 27). Eventually, the composition used in Ref 28 was found to be very similar to this work. The respective material properties are summarized in Tables 3 and 4. Properties of molten 316L stainless steel vary as the particles go through phase change from liquid to solid. For surface tension, the effect of chemical composition is even more important. Specifically, variations in the percentage of sulfur content from zero to a few hundred ppm (i.e., microgram per gram) can reduce the surface tension by about 0.4 N/m (see Ref 29–33). It should also be noted that surface tension measurements for liquid metals are typically associated with a high level of difficulty and uncertainty. Nevertheless, the reported values in the literature are more or less in the same range. In the work of Brooks and Mills Ref 30, surface tension of stainless steel 316L was measured for various sulfur concentrations and temperatures. Here, we interpolated the value of surface tension based on the results of Brooks and Mills Ref 30 for a sulfur concentration of 160 ppm (see Table 2). Similarly, a very large value was also assigned to surface tension below solidus temperature to mimic the condition of a solid material (see Table 5).
