Experimental Studies
A set of experiments were performed in a systematic manner to study the effects of various operating conditions on gas dynamics, flow patterns, atomization process, and particle size distribution. Initially, the effect of atomization pressure on ‘‘suction pressure’’ was studied. Here, suction pressure is defined as the (gauge) pressure that forms on the surface of the nozzle due the flow of atomization gas (see
Fig. 2). As discussed later, this pressure is typically negative and, together with gravity, helps the melt to be drawn out of the nozzle. For this purpose, a digital pressure sensor (Model DP-102-J, Panasonic) was used, as shown in Fig. 4. For these tests, the original nozzle was removed from the atomizer port. Instead, a dummy nozzle was made out of plastic, with a similar geometry to the original nozzle, and the pressure sensor was implemented into it. This assembly (as shown in Fig. 4, right) was then inserted in to the atomizer port (Fig. 4, left) to mimic a similar environment to when the original nozzle is in place. For each test, initially, the atomization pressure was adjusted to the specified value (i.e., 3 MPa, 4.5 MPa, 5.5 MPa, or 6 MPa). Then, the gas flow was started for 5 to 7 seconds, and the pressure was recorded after the reading on the sensor became stable. In order to ensure the reliability of the experimental results, each group of tests was repeated three times, and the results were averaged. It should be noted that due to equipment limitations, gas leakage was inevitable during the tests which may lead to some errors in the results. Furthermore, due to the absence of any melt flow and elevated temperature, the pressures measured with this method may not closely reflect the actual pressure in realtime operation of the atomizer. Nevertheless, these tests provide a valuable insight into how varying inlet pressure affects the suction pressure, as discussed in the Results section. Next, the effects of gas pressure on the atomization process and particle size distribution were investigated in real-time operation of the instrument. In these tests, two different nozzle diameters were used (i.e., 4 mm and 4.6 mm). These diameters are typically used for metal powder production using the HPGA system described in Fig. 2. For each nozzle, the atomization gas pressure was set to 3 MPa, 4 MPa, 5.5 MPa, and 6 MPa in separate experiments and particles were collected. The general operating conditions for these experiments are listed below.
