The melt flow rate was measured indirectly by dividing the weight of the feed material by duration of the atomization process. Initially, the weight of the 316L stainless steel feed powder was weighed before being poured into the crucible. After finishing the atomization process, the weight of the collected powder (see Fig. 5) was measured again. The difference between the two would be the ‘‘uncollected particles’’ which typically includes the evaporated part of the material, the melt attached to the inner wall of the crucible, the melt that drips from the nozzle in advance of the atomization process (Fig. 5), and the powder attached to the inner walls of the collection tank. Finally, the size distribution of the collected powder was determined by successive sieving of the collected particles as discussed in the Results section.
Results and Discussion
Gas Flow Dynamics
Initially, simulations were performed without injecting any particles into the computational domain to study various gas dynamics phenomena. Figure 6 shows the respective contours of velocity for various atomization pressures. This figure shows the general characteristics of gas flow as it expands from a highly pressurized chamber, featuring a series of sequential shocks. This behavior is consistent with theoretical description of compressible flows and jets in the literature. As the gas enters the atomization chamber through the narrow annulus formed between the nozzle and body of the atomizer, it gives rise to a supersonic, turbulent flow inside the atomization chamber. The gas flow reaches a Mach number of unity at the end of the annular channel and subsequently expands through a series of waves and oblique shocks downstream of the nozzle to match the pressure in the atomization chamber. As can be seen from
Fig. 6, the first shock is the strongest, while subsequent shocks become weaker through further expansion of the gas. Clearly, increasing the atomization gas pressure leads to larger and stronger shock diamonds. As the pressure is increased from 3 MPa to 6 MPa, the maximum velocity at the first shock increases from 717 to 733 m/s with a corresponding Mach number of 4.7 to 5.5, respectively. As an example, Fig. 7 shows that at 3.5 MP, the gas flow separates at the edge of the nozzle lip to create a recirculation zone in front of the nozzle. This recirculation zone is distinguished from the high velocity stream by the sonic lines for which the Mach number is one (Ref 7). The gas in the middle of the recirculation zone moves toward the nozzle and then turns outward radially toward the edge. The gas in the recirculation zone is pushed inward by a thin turbulent layer as it comes into contact with the sonic boundary (Ref 6). When the gas velocity drops to zero along the axis, it marks the end of the recirculation zone which is called the stagnation point, as shown in Fig. 7. Figure 8 shows the experimental results of pressure measurement on the surface of the nozzle, as the atomization pressure is increased. Similarly, Fig. 8 (top right) shows the simulated variation of average pressure on the surface of the nozzle as well as nozzle lip. It can be seen that as the atomization pressure is gradually increased, the gauge static pressure on both the nozzle and the nozzle lip decreases. This trend is in agreement with the experiments. Quantitatively, the values measured experimentally fall between the pressure on the nozzle and the pressure on the lip. Figure 8 (bottom) also shows the radial variation of pressure along the surface of the nozzle (i.e., nozzle ? lip). It can be seen that the pressure is higher on the nozzle outlet at the center and gradually decreases as we move away from the nozzle in the radial direction. At the edge, the pressure suddenly rises due to the flow of the atomization gas that is coming out of the annular channel.
