Melt Atomization
As the high-speed gas flows out of the gas supply in a close-coupled atomizer, it drags the metal liquid exiting the nozzle radially outward along the lip of the nozzle to form a thin liquid film. Therefore, this type of atomizer design is called ‘‘pre-filming.’’ The thin liquid film is then torn by the gas flow, first to ligaments and then to tiny droplets. Pre-filming is the preferred operating regime for producing fine powders. When the melt flow rate is too high, the filming effect will be partially lost and some of the metal might be entrained into the low velocity wake (i.e., recirculation zone). This, in turn, creates coarser powders with undesirable flakes. Table 6 summarizes the values of melt flow rate measured experimentally as well as the D50 (the particle diameter that separates the collected particles in half) of the collected particles for various inlet gas pressures. Figure 11 and 12 show the particle size distributions for the 4 mm and 4.6 mm nozzles, respectively, which were determined by successive sieving of the collected particles and then weighing each bin. Looking at Table 6, it can be seen that as the atomization gas pressure is increased from 3 to 6 MPa, the melt
flow rate is decreased. This is while the suction pressure (Fig. 8) was seen to increase—based on both the experimental and simulation results—which should essentially lead to a higher melt flow rate by pulling the melt out of the nozzle (i.e., pulling effect). From these observations, it is clear that aside from gravity and suction pressure, additional phenomena affect the flow of melt at the nozzle outlet. One of these important phenomena is the effect of shear force on the nozzle surface. As discussed previously (Fig. 7 and the related discussions), a recirculation zone forms in front of the nozzle, the size and intensity of which is affected by the atomization pressure and nozzle geometry. From Fig. 7, it can be seen that, at the center, the gas flows toward the nozzle and then continues radially toward the edge. Figure 8 (bottom) clearly shows this effect where a stagnation point (with relatively higher pressure) forms on the nozzle outlet. As a result of this flow pattern, the melt flow rate and performance of the atomizer are determined by the interaction of pressure, shear and gravity forces on the surface of the nozzle. A schematic diagram of these forces is shown in Fig. 13. The contribution of each of these forces to the melt flow rate is discussed in the following paragraphs.
