As seen from the experimental measurements and simulations, the pressure on the surface of the nozzle is usually smaller than atmospheric pressure (or the pressure within the melt crucible). Although simulations show (see Fig. 8) that when atomization pressure is decreased below 2 MPa, this pressure becomes higher than the atmospheric pressure and can effectively compete against gravity to prevent the melt from coming out of the nozzle. This effect has also been reported by others (Ref 34, 35). Both the experiments and simulations (Fig. 8) indicate that by increasing the atomization pressure, the pressure on the surface of the nozzle can be further decreased below atmospheric pressure. While this creates a pulling effect that should essentially increase the melt flow rate, the opposite is seen based on Table 6. This is due to shear forces that the gas exerts on the nozzle as it flows along its surface in the radial direction toward the edge (Fig. 13, top). As shown by the simulations in Fig. 13, the magnitude of the shear stress (and therefore the shear force) decreases when the atomization pressure is increased from 2 MPa to 6 MPa. This trend is in agreement with the decrease in melt flow rate as the atomization pressure is increased. Thus, it may be concluded that shear forces play the dominant role on determining the melt flow rate in close
coupled atomizers. That is, as the melt moves downward due to gravity, shear forces constantly remove melt from the nozzle and direct it toward the edge to be atomized. By decreasing the atomization pressure, the melt flow rate can be increased. However, it should be reiterated that if the atomization pressure is decreased excessively, it might cause the pressure on the nozzle to become positive and block the flow of melt. Based on Fig. 13, it is also worth noting that the shear force suddenly increases at 7 MPa which might lead to an increased melt flow rate compared to lower pressures. It would be interesting to experimentally confirm this observation in the future. As explained in Sect. ‘‘General Particle Motion’’, to simulate the atomization process based on the method proposed by Ref 2, 3 and 20, 10 classes of primary droplets were injected at the edge of the nozzle lip. The diameter of these droplets was in the range of 0.4–4 mm, with a mean diameter of 2.2 mm, based on a Rosin-Rammler distribution. Similarly, for the 4.6 mm nozzle, 10 classes of droplet sizes were injected in the range of 0.46–4.6 mm with a mean diameter of 2.5 mm. Figure 14, 15 and 16 shows the simulated velocity, temperature, and particle diameter distribution, respectively, for various atomization pressures when particles are injected for
