Therefore, while it is unlikely to collect heavy particles away from the axis, the likelihood of finding smaller particles in every region of the spray is higher. In other words, the spray is a blend of light particles in the core and at the periphery of the spray jet, and heavy particles which are mostly limited to the core of the spray. Figure 20 shows the simulated particle size distribution for the 4.6 mm nozzle at various atomization pressures. During the simulation, the data for these particles were recorded on file whenever a particle exited the computational domain through the pressure outlet boundaries (Fig. 3). To extract these data from the recorded files, calculate the D50 for particles, and generate the histograms, a program was developed in MATLAB. Initially, the program read the particle data, sorted the particles based on their diameter, and put them in various bins based on their ‘‘mass.’’ Here, D50 is obtained by interpolation based on the cumulative mass percentage. Additionally, the program is able to generate a frequency distribution based on the ‘‘number’’ of particles (instead of their mass) in each bin. The D50 (i.e., average diameter) associated with this distribution is denoted as D50N in this work. Therefore, the number-based average diameter D50N is always smaller than the mass-based D50. Figure 21 shows a comparison between the simulated particle distribution (number-based D50N) for the 4 mm nozzle at 4.5 MPa atomization pressure and the one provided by the manufacturer of the atomization system (Fig. 2). A laser particle size analyzer (Bettersizer 3000 plus, Bettersize Instruments, China) was used by the manufacturer to measure the particle size distribution that the system typically generates. The experimental and
