Conclusion
The purpose of this work was to investigate the effect of various operating conditions on the performance of a highpressure gas atomizer through experiments and computer simulations and to gain a better understanding of various mechanisms that determine the quality of final product in the application of these atomizers for metal powder synthesis. The findings of this work can be summarized as follows:
• Simulations show that a recirculation zone forms in front of the nozzle. The flow pattern, intensity, and size of this zone depend on the design of the atomizer, atomization gas pressure, and melt flow rate. • The performance of the atomizer is determined by the interaction of three forces on the surface of the nozzle: gravity, suction pressure, and gas shear force. Gravity causes the molten metal to flow out of the nozzle. The suction pressure, which is usually negative, helps the melt to be pulled out of the nozzle. However, if the atomization gas pressure is reduced excessively, this pressure can become positive and impede the flow of melt. The shear force exerted by the gas drags the melt along the surface of the nozzle toward the edge where the melt meets the atomization gas, ruptures, and turns into finer droplets. • Both simulations and experiments show a negative correlation between the suction pressure and melt flow rate, but a positive correlation between shear force and gravity with melt flow rate. Therefore, the shear force
along with gravity are concluded to have the dominant role on determining the melt flow rate. • It was seen that increasing the atomization gas pressure decreases the melt flow rate, results in finer particles (as characterized by smaller D50s), and cools the particles faster. • Simulations showed that the spray is a blend of light particles in its core and at the periphery of the spray jet, while heavy particles are mostly limited to the core of the spray. Therefore, it is expected to see heavier particles at the center of the powder collection chamber. Particles exiting the computational domain were seen to have more or less similar temperatures due to the fact that they were mostly going through solidification. Most of the particles were found to be focused at a distance of 7.2 mm away from the central axis. Particles with diameters larger than the average particle size were seen to travel closer to the axis while smaller particles travel farther away. • It was found that decreasing surface tension or increasing the size of primary droplets lead to an smaller D50.
Further studies are required to improve the limitations associated with modeling high-pressure gas atomizer for metal powder synthesis. But the conclusions provided herein show that a combination of computer simulations and experimental measurement can be used as a valuable tool to analyze the performance and behavior of highpressure metal atomizers. This, in turn, can be used to improve the design of these systems and lead to a better
